{{smaller|the triangulations of different countries the figure of the earth is ascertained. See cut under ''base-line.''}}

'''triangulator''' (trī-ang′gū-lā-tọr), ''n.'' [< ''triangulate'' + ''-or''<sup>1</sup>.] One who performs the work of triangulation in a trigonometrical survey.

'''trianguloid''' (trī-ang′gū-loid), ''a.'' [< L. ''triangulum'', a triangle, + Gr. εἶδος, form.] Somewhat triangular in shape.

{{smaller block|A ''trianguloid'' space.
{{right|''H. Spencer.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}

'''Triangulum''' (trī-ang′gū-lum), ''n.'' [L.: see ''triangle.''] An ancient northern constellation in the form of the letter delta (Δ). It has one star of the third magnitude.—{{smaller|'''Triangulum Australe''' (the Southern Triangle), a southern constellation, added by Petrus Theodori in the fifteenth century, south of Ara. It contains one star of the second and two of the third magnitude.—'''Triangulum Minus''' (the Lesser Triangle), a constellation introduced by Hevelius in 1690, immediately south of Triangulum. It is no longer in use.}}

'''triantelope, triantulope''' (trī-an′tē-lōp, -tū-lōp), ''n.'' [A corruption of ''tarantula'', simulating ''antelope.''] A tarantula. [Australia.]

{{smaller block|Tarantulas, or large spiders (as the bushmen call them, ''triantulopes''), . . . come crawling down the sides of the tent in wet weather.}}

